---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DeviceScreen from '../../components/DeviceScreen.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
import wdwScreen from '../../assets/device/91-wdw.png';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Wet/Dry/Wet Routing"
  description="Build an Ardor wet/dry/wet rig with a mono dry lane and a stereo wet lane."
  lede="Wet/Dry/Wet routing runs two amp paths from the same guitar input. Ardor mixes the dry lane with a stereo wet lane at the output."
>
  <h2>How the lanes work</h2>
  <DeviceScreen
    src={wdwScreen}
    alt="Wet dry wet editor with a Dry lane containing a Clean Twin NAM and cabinet IR, and a Wet lane containing a British Crunch NAM and digital delay, joined at the stereo output."
    caption="The WDW editor shows the mono Dry lane above the stereo Wet lane. Both lanes join at the stereo output."
    sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 52rem"
  />
  <p>
    Ardor splits the mono guitar input into two processed lanes. Each lane has its own Neural
    Amp Modeler (NAM) block. The Dry lane is mono and is usually centred. The Wet lane keeps
    a stereo output through its time effects. Ardor mixes both lane outputs to the stereo
    output.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Lane</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Blocks</th><th>Mix control</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Dry</strong></td>
        <td>Mono amp path without stereo time effects.</td>
        <td>NAM, optional cab, dynamics, EQ, distortion, and wah.</td>
        <td>Level and pan.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Wet</strong></td>
        <td>Amp path with stereo time effects.</td>
        <td>NAM, optional cab, modulation, delay, reverb, and stereo blocks.</td>
        <td>Level and stereo width.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      The Dry lane is still processed by its amp model. WDW does not add an unprocessed
      guitar signal to the mix. A cabinet IR is optional when the NAM capture already
      includes a cabinet sound.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build a WDW preset</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the preset in the Manager and choose <strong>Global</strong>.</li>
    <li>Set <strong>Topology</strong> to <strong>Wet dry wet</strong>.</li>
    <li>Choose a NAM block for the Dry lane and another for the Wet lane.</li>
    <li>Add an optional cabinet after each NAM when you need a separate cab IR.</li>
    <li>Add drive or tone blocks to Dry, and stereo effects to Wet.</li>
    <li>Adjust each lane's level. Pan Dry or set Wet width to balance the stereo image.</li>
    <li>Save the preset and choose <strong>Apply</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Each lane requires exactly one enabled NAM block and supports up to ten blocks. A lane can
    be switched off while you compare the mix. Both lanes use the preset's shared input gain,
    output gain, and safety limit. Scenes can store lane enable, level, pan, and width values.
  </p>

  <h2>WDW and Dual Rig</h2>
  <p>
    Use WDW when you want one mono amp contribution alongside a stereo effects path. Use
    <a href={url('/docs/dual-rig')}>Dual Rig</a> when you want separate left and right chains
    that feed the left and right outputs. The two layouts have different lane rules and are
    separate preset topologies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read <a href={url('/docs/signal-chain')}>Signal Chain &amp; Routing</a> for the other
    routing options, or <a href={url('/docs/presets')}>Presets &amp; Storage</a> for preset
    files and version details.
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>Advanced: preset format</summary>
    <p>
      WDW presets use version 3. A preset with scenes uses version 4. Set
      <code>routing</code> to <code>wdw</code>, keep the top-level <code>blocks</code> array
      empty, and store each lane under <code>wdw.dry</code> and <code>wdw.wet</code>. The
      dry lane stores <code>levelDb</code>, <code>pan</code>, and <code>enabled</code>. The
      wet lane stores <code>levelDb</code>, <code>width</code>, and <code>enabled</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each lane can have one cab after its NAM. Wet time effects must follow the NAM and any
      cab. Nested Dual Amp and Dual Rig blocks are not supported inside WDW lanes.
    </p>
  </details>
</DocsLayout>
